    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park/Casual-Academic-Register---College-of-Humanities--Arts-and-Social-Sciences_JR0000000090": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Register – College of Humanities, Arts and Social Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2024-11-18\nPart time\n\nAbout Flinders Our bold vision, captured in our Strategic Plan: Making a Difference: The 2025 Agenda, is to be internationally recognised as a world leader in research, an innovator in contemporary education, and the source of Australia’s most enterprising graduates. To realise this ambition, we recently made a significant organisational change to a six College structure with a professional staff and services alignment. We recognise the key to our success is exceptional people and we’re seeking an outstanding individual to join the leadership team of our transformed university. Casual Register Flinders University has a number of Casual Registers as a means of expressions of interest for the purpose of casual work, should such opportunities arise. How to Register: Upload your current CV/Resumé detailing your qualifications and experience. You may choose to upload a Cover Letter of no more than 1 page to support your CV/Resumé. Your application will be acknowledged by an automated e-mail once submitted. If a casual opportunity is available and your application is considered suitable, the relevant hiring manager will contact you for a further discussion. Please note, only suitable applicants will be contacted. Applying to the Casual Register does not guarantee employment. Further Information: Pursuant to Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA) this position may be deemed prescribed.  It is an inherent requirement of a prescribed position that the successful candidate maintains a current Working With Children Check which is satisfactory to the University. Please contact Recruitment@Flinders  by email at recruitment@flinders.edu.au  should you have any queries regarding Casual opportunities."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Tonsley/Cisco-Research-Chair-in-Defence-Capability-and-Security_JR0000017614": {
      "title": "Cisco Research Chair in Defence Capability and Security",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full Time | Five-year contract Academic Level E | AU$217,876 | 17% Superannuation Location: Tonsley / Kaurna Country /Australia View Position Description View Candidate Brochure A Position with Impact: Flinders University is at the frontline of defence-oriented research and is seeking an inaugural Cisco Research Chair in Defence Capability and Security. Based in Adelaide, Australia’s Defence State, and home to some of the nation’s most significant defence projects, investment and sovereign capability development, this is a nationally significant opportunity to lead research at the intersection of digital technology, defence and sovereign capability. With direct access to Cisco’s technology and global research network, you will be positioned at the centre of an environment where Defence, industry, technology and government are actively shaping Australia’s future capability.   Working in partnership with Cisco, the Australian Defence Force, the Department of Defence and Australia’s sovereign defence industry, you will establish and lead an applied research programme that translates emerging technologies into real-world defence capability. You will have the opportunity to shape research that advances AUKUS priorities, strengthens critical infrastructure resilience and builds Australia’s sovereign defence capability.   In this position, you will: Lead with impact: Establish and shape a high-profile applied research programme spanning AI, autonomous systems, advanced manufacturing and/or cyber security, translating emerging technology into capability for Defence and industry. Work at the forefront of innovation: Partner directly with Cisco, the Australian Defence Force, the Department of Defence and sovereign defence industry, with access to Cisco’s next-generation networking, security, AI and IoT technologies and global research network. Build what comes next: Grow a team of researchers and HDR candidates, attract major research and industry investment, influence national conversations and help develop the sovereign technology capability Australia needs for the future.   This is a flagship academic appointment established under the Flinders-Cisco partnership to advance defence capabilities through world-leading research innovation and impact.     About You: You are an established researcher, technology leader or senior defence practitioner with a strong track record in defence, national security or digital technology.   You will bring: A doctoral qualification in a relevant discipline OR equivalent achievement at a senior practitioner level in defence industries or national security Demonstrated expertise in advanced manufacturing, AI, autonomous systems (maritime) or cyber security for defence. A strong record of research, industry or government-funded programmes and/or competitive funding. The ability to translate technical innovation into real-world capability outcomes. Established networks across Defence, government, industry and/or the broader technology ecosystem. Exceptional communication and leadership skills, with the credibility to engage across technical, Defence, industry and policy environments.   International and domestic candidates are welcome to apply. Eligibility and willingness to obtain an Australian Government security clearance at Negative Vetting Level 1 or higher is highly desirable.   A Partnership Built for Innovation: Flinders University and Cisco have established one of Australia’s leading university-industry partnerships in digital innovation and applied research. Since 2015, the partnership has evolved into an integrated ecosystem spanning world-class research, industry collaboration, student innovation, workforce development and translational impact. Through a complementary national network of Cisco Chairs, Innovation Central Adelaide, and the National Industry Innovation Network (NIIN), the partnership provides a nationally connected innovation ecosystem that accelerates research impact and translation, industry collaboration, talent development and the adoption of new technologies to address complex industry challenges. As the Cisco Research Chair, you will be embedded within this dynamic ecosystem, connecting world-class research with Cisco technology, industry expertise and real-world opportunities to deliver impact in Australia and beyond. Why Flinders University? They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders? Recognised as the top 2% university worldwide, with research income soaring to $133 million in research contracts and grants. 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre‑employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. This includes a Gender‑Based Violence declaration, in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender‑Based Violence (2025), and a Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check. An NCCHC disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.  How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV and a three-page Suitability Statement addressing the key position capabilities of the position description . For more information regarding this position, we strongly encourage you to please conta ct Professor Michael Blumenstein via Cse.operations@flinders.edu.au . Applications to be submitted before 10:00 pm (ACDT) on Wednesday, 21 October 2026. Please note, late applications and applications sent via agencies will not be accepted . At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 21 Oct 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Centre-for-Remote-Health-Alice-Springs/Lecturer-in-Remote-Health--Nursing-_JR0000017148": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Remote Health (Nursing)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Fixed Term until 31 December 2027 | Up to full-time hours available (0.6 to 1.0 FTE negotiable) Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 p.a. Location: Alice Springs - Arrernte View Position Description About the Role Join our Rural and Remote Health team as a Lecturer in Remote Health (Nursing), where you will play a key role in preparing and supporting the future health workforce for remote and Indigenous communities. This teaching-focused position contributes to excellence in nursing and remote health education through innovative, student-centred learning experiences. Working within a multidisciplinary team across the Northern Territory, you will deliver high-quality teaching, develop contemporary curriculum, and help equip graduates with the skills and knowledge required to thrive in remote acute and primary healthcare settings. Key responsibilities include: Delivering engaging and innovative teaching across nursing and remote health topics, using contemporary educational practices and technologies to enhance student success. Developing, coordinating and reviewing curriculum and learning materials, including undertaking Topic Coordinator responsibilities as required. Providing academic guidance, mentoring and professional advice to support students' learning, career development and readiness for practice. Building and maintaining strong partnerships with healthcare providers, professional bodies and communities to enhance student learning opportunities and workforce outcomes in rural and remote health.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035, through directly contributing to the strategic driver of Student Centricity. By delivering innovative, high-quality nursing and remote health education, supporting student success, and preparing graduates for impactful careers in remote and Indigenous healthcare settings, the role helps create meaningful outcomes for students, communities and the health workforce.   About You You are a passionate and experienced nursing educator with a commitment to student success and excellence in teaching. Bringing contemporary professional expertise and strong tertiary teaching capability, you thrive in collaborative environments and are motivated by the opportunity to prepare the next generation of health professionals for practice in rural, remote and Indigenous healthcare settings. You combine innovative teaching approaches with outstanding communication and relationship-building skills to create engaging and supportive learning experiences. You have a strong track record of designing and delivering innovative, student-centred learning experiences, including the effective use of online and educational technologies. You possess excellent interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships with students, colleagues, industry partners and community stakeholders. You bring experience in curriculum development, academic quality processes and coordinating teaching activities, alongside an ongoing commitment to professional engagement and continuous improvement.   Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT At Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT , our aim is to be a hub for innovative health research, education, and workforce development across the Northern Territory.  We engage with students, community, and stakeholders to provide critical programs, such as the Northern Territory Medical Program, Paramedicine, Rural Clinical School, and the University Department of Rural Health. Our postgraduate education pathways (Remote Health Practice program), provides workforce development through Indigenous health and clinical skills training, and we have a community driven research program led by researchers committed to improving health outcomes. We work alongside Poche SA+NT focussing on Indigenous leadership capacity development and developing the Indigenous health workforce.  All activities are driven by our values which focus on the long-term and short-term needs of the NT community. Life at Flinders We're transforming and investing in people and facilities to create contemporary, stimulating, and satisfying learning and work environments that reflect our core values of excellence, innovation, courage, and integrity. Flinders is refocusing its strategic priorities with the aim of elevating its performance to be a top ten Australian university, and amongst the top 1% in the world. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice 6 weeks annual leave (Northern Territory)  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment A current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check which is satisfactory to the University will be required by Flinders University before the successful applicant can commence in this position. A criminal record will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Please note that you may be required to provide evidence that you are up to date with COVID-19 vaccinations, in line with the Flinders University  COVID-19 Vaccination Policy   Northern Territory Government legislation (the Care and Protection of Children Act 2007) now requires all individuals in child related employment or whose duties involve, or are likely to involve, contact with a child, to hold a valid 'Working with Children Clearance' (Ochre Card). This position involves child related work and is assessed as requiring a Working with Children Clearance/Ochre Card. A valid Northern Territory (NT) Driver’s License is an essential requirement of this position and must be maintained for the duration of employment. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged, and can, as far as practicably possible, participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, including being accompanied by an accredited assistance animal with public access rights as per the Disability Policy , please advise the contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Kim Pearce Applications to be submitted before 10:00 pm (ACDT) Sunday, 25 October 2026. Please note, late applications and applications sent via agencies will not be accepted . At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 25 Oct 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Lecturer-Lecturer-in-Midwifery_JR0000017694": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer/Lecturer in Midwifery",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Continuing | Full-Time or Part-Time available Academic Level B or C | $115,871 - $162,619 p.a. pro-rata Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description – Lecturer Teaching Specialist (Clinical Practitioner) View Position Description – Senior Lecturer Teaching Specialist (Clinical Practitioner) About the Role/s Lecturer The Lecturer in Midwifery - Teaching Specialist (Clinical Practitioner), will contribute to excellence in teaching and learning across a range of midwifery topics. The lecturer will play a key role in developing and delivering high-quality, innovative teaching through lectures, tutorials, clinical placements, and contemporary digital strategies. This includes creating course materials, coordinating placements, and conducting assessments. The role also requires ongoing clinical or community-based practice to maintain current skills and knowledge. In addition, the incumbent will provide informal leadership by supporting academic staff, coordinating workflows, and ensuring high standards aligned with university policies and practices. Senior Lecturer The Senior Lecturer in Midwifery – Teaching Specialist (Clinical Practitioner), will provide leadership in delivering excellence in teaching and learning across the suite of Midwifery topics and courses in the College of Healthcare and Enablement, that enhance the student experience. The role will provide educational leadership; curriculum design and review; delivery innovation; advancements in education technologies; and leadership in ensuring internal and external compliance and accreditation of courses and continuous quality improvement of university-level education. The Senior Lecturer will also be expected to concurrently engage in practice in a clinical or community setting to support maintenance of contemporary practice, knowledge and skills. Please refer to the attached position descriptions for a comprehensive list of roles and responsibilities. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through a commitment to a student-centred ethos and to developing creative, enterprising, career-ready graduates prepared to become lifelong contributors to society.   About You The Lecturer will have demonstrated excellence in tertiary teaching across classroom, simulation and/or clinical settings, including placement coordination. You will have a proven ability to independently contribute to the development, delivery and evaluation of innovative teaching and learning strategies, including student assessment, and an understanding of the nexus between teaching and learning, research, scholarship and practice. Additionally, as the Senior Lecturer, you will have significant and sustained evidence of excellence in tertiary teaching, including experience in simulation and/or clinical settings. You will have a strong track record of developing, delivering and evaluating innovative teaching and learning strategies across classroom and clinical education environments, together with a demonstrated commitment to teaching scholarship and professional development in higher education. You will have a strong understanding of the nexus between teaching and learning, research, scholarship and practice, together with experience coordinating and/or leading the teaching activities of colleagues. In these roles you will also be able to demonstrate:   A completion of a PhD, or other relevant and equivalent qualification and/or experience. Hold and maintain a current membership with Nursing and Midwifery Board of Australia & Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA). Relevant and contemporary clinical experience as an accredited Midwife. An understanding of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to Midwifery. Excellent oral and written communication skills and interpersonal skills, including the demonstrated ability to establish effective relationships with staff, students and industry.     College of Health and Enablement    At the College of Health and Enablement, we aim to be an innovator in contemporary education producing a safer and more resilient health and social care workforce. Our mission is to improve lives and empower communities, enabling better health and caring in the world through an exceptional student learning experience. At the College, our research mission is about co-creating new knowledge which promotes wellbeing, and better care and support, to individuals and communities across the lifespan. We work proactively with industry partners and professional bodies leading to more employable and innovative graduates.      Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy      Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include:   Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. In addition, it is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA). SA: Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.    How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the View Position Description – Lecturer Teaching Specialist (Clinical Practitioner) View Position Description – Senior Lecturer Teaching Specialist (Clinical Practitioner)  Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Professor Chris Barr Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 22 Oct 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
