    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Research-Contracts-Officer_JR0000017800": {
      "title": "Senior Research Contracts Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Continuing I Full Time  Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $94,456 - $100,710 pa | 17% Super Higher Education Officer Level 7 | $102,589 - $113,561 pa | 17% Super HEO6 Position Description | HEO7 Position Description Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country About the Role: We are seeking a motivated and skilled professional to join our Research Development and Support team. This unique opportunity is being offered at either the HEO6 (Research Contracts Officer) or HEO7 (Senior Research Contracts Officer) level , depending on the successful candidate's experience and qualifications. In the Research Contracts Officer position, your duties will include (but are not limited to): Providing valued support to the Flinders research community in the administration and processing of contracts and agreements for externally sponsored projects, and providing expertise, customer service and timely advice. Reviewing and negotiating the terms and conditions of research related agreements with industry, government, and other research institutions. Working with the other members of the Research Development and Support team to identify, define and resolve issues related to sponsored project contracting activities. Reviewing and providing advice to researchers on contractual obligations, special conditions or restrictions, or programmatic and other deliverables required by specific funding schemes. In addition to the above, in the Senior Research Contracts Officer position, your duties will include (but are not limited to): The administration and processing of strategic, complex and escalated research contracts and agreements. Assisting with supervisory responsibilities, including developing and mentoring members of the Research Contracts team, and providing backfill for management where required. Identifying and implementing innovative process improvements in contract administration and management.   About You To be successful in this role, you will have a strong background in contract administration, drafting, or legal review, preferably within a higher education, government, or research-intensive environment. You possess exceptional communication and negotiation skills, with a proven ability to solve complex problems and balance legal risk against strategic institutional goals. To be successful, you will also require: Excellent time management skills and demonstrated ability to prioritise tasks to meet tight deadlines. Contemporary knowledge of compliance requirements for major grant funding schemes including ARC and NHMRC. Ability to work independently and as part of a team, exercise initiative and judgment in interpreting and applying a range of policies and procedures and work with a broad range of stakeholders, such as academic and professional staff.   Life at Flinders Flinders University is driven by a bold purpose: to change lives and change the world. Through Agenda 2035 – Impact. With Purpose. , we are expanding the scale, reach, and ambition of our research to deliver meaningful outcomes for communities, industry, and global health systems. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Wide range of professional development activities and services We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia). Support future initiatives like the Flinders HealthCARE Centre Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.    How to Apply and Information As well as a CV, you are required to submit a Suitability Statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the following Key Position Capabilities of the position description: Research Contracts Officer : Demonstrated expertise in research contract management and administration, including drafting, negotiating, reviewing and administration of research contracts and other related documents in a complex environment. Well-developed to high level of attention to detail. Ability to work independently and as part of a team, exercise initiative and judgment in interpreting and applying a range of policies and procedures and work with a broad range of stakeholders, such as academic and professional staff. High level verbal and written communication skills. Senior Research Contracts Officer : Demonstrated experience in the management and administration of complex and strategic research contracts, including drafting, negotiating, reviewing, relationship management, and administration of research contracts and other related documents in a complex environment. Demonstrated well developed to high level verbal and written communication skills including high level attention to detail. Demonstrated ability to identify and implement innovative process improvements in contract administration and management. Ability to mentor, coach and develop professional staff. Demonstrated ability to work independently and as part of a team, exercise initiative and judgment in interpreting and applying a range of policies and procedures and working with a broad range of stakeholders, such as academic and professional staff. For more information regarding this position, please contact Sophie Trabilsie . Certification National Police Check - Australian Federal Police, Working With Children Check - Government of South Australia Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 01 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Lecturer-Medical-Education--Clinical-Practice-and-Simulation-Lead-_JR0000017794": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer Medical Education (Clinical Practice and Simulation Lead)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Exclusive Continuing | Full-time Position (36.75 hours per week) Academic Level C | $141,370 - $162,619 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description   About the Role   We are seeking a Senior Lecturer, Medical Education (Clinical Practice and Simulation Lead) provide leadership in delivering excellence in teaching and learning across the suite of medical education topics and courses prominently in the Doctor of Medicine but inclusive of other college courses with a clinical component. This new, continuing position is well suited to a distinguished practitioner who has perfected the translation of clinical knowledge into practice, and is passionate about nurturing the next generation of Medicine students in a hand- on, simulation environment. The successful candidate will primary be responsible for making a significant and wide-ranging contribution to leadership in teaching, curriculum development and scholarship of teaching using innovations in teaching practices and technologies to deliver excellence in teaching. You will also have the opportunity to: Lead the planning, scheduling and evaluation of Clinical skills practice and simulated learning teaching. Play a lead role in the review, renewal, development and design of curricula and courses that are attractive to existing and new cohorts of students and that nurture creativity and critical-thinking skills. Provide support, academic counselling and professional advice to students to support their studies, professional development and career progression. Provide educational leadership and dissemination of best practice, ensuring awareness and utilisation of contemporary education principles. This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters , by 2035 through the Strategic Driver of Students First. The primary focus of this role is to design and integrate clinical skills, clinical reasoning and patient assessment activities across curriculum to in turn achieve a diverse and supportive learning environment that sparks curiosity, builds confidence and enables every student to succeed in real-world, on the job settings.   About You   You are a passionate and experienced medical educator with a strong commitment to student success, teaching excellence and continuous improvement in higher education that is underpinned by your experience and expertise in hands-on simulated-focused learning. You bring demonstrated leadership in curriculum design, leadership and innovative teaching practices, alongside the ability to build meaningful relationships with students, colleagues and external partners. With a health professional background and a contemporary approach to learning and teaching, you are motivated to shape the next generation of healthcare professionals through excellence in education and collaboration.   To be successful in this position, you also be able to demonstrate: Evidence of application of pedagogical theory and contemporary educational practice relevant to the discipline, in addition to substantial evidence of teaching at a tertiary level, inclusive of simulation and/or clinical setting. Demonstrated capacity to design and integrate clinical skills, clinical reasoning and patient assessment activities across curriculum. Relevant health professional qualification and current registration with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (Ahpra). PHD, Tertiary Qualifications and/or equivalent professional experience in Health.   College of Medicine and Public Health            At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document. This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Fiona Wise . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 29 Oct 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Change-Management-Lead_JR0000017780": {
      "title": "Change Management Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Fixed term until April 2029 Higher Education Officer Level 9 | $133,860 - $143,239 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Flinders HealthCare Centre The Flinders HealthCARE Centre (FHC) is a multidisciplinary education, research, and healthcare facility located at the heart of Flinders Village and co-funded by the Australian Government and Flinders University. Scheduled to open in 2029, FHC will support the delivery of approximately 1,300 additional health graduates and up to 10,000 additional episodes of health and community care annually, bringing together education, research, and health and community services to enable new models of care, innovative use of technology, and transformative learning. About the Program  The Program supports the establishment and activation of the FHC, working across the Colleges of Health and Enablement (CHE), Medicine and Public Health (CMPH), and Human Sciences and Culture (CHSC). With support from central services, the Activation team is structured across four workstreams and five enabling streams to support the design of an innovative interprofessional curriculum, underpinned by a newly defined clinical services model and contemporary simulation practices. The Program will also focus on co-designing health services in partnership with government and industry partners. About the Role Join us as our Change Management Lead for the activation of the Flinders HealthCare Centre, offering an exciting opportunity to be a part of a high performing project team who are shaping the development of the iconic, state of the art Flinders HealthCare Centre. Reporting to the Program Director, and working closely with the Associate Director, Change and Strategy, in the People and Culture portfolio, the Change Management Lead will ensure change strategies and plans align with the organisational objectives and the University's Change Management Framework. The incumbent will bring significant experience and sound knowledge in change management and communication, with the ability to foster cultural alignment during the transition into the new Flinders HealthCARE Centre building. The incumbent will lead change management programs and develop comprehensive plans to ensure the FHC Activation Program’s change initiatives are successfully implemented. The role is also responsible for: Overseeing, gathering and analysing data, assessing the impact of change, recommending interventions and mitigations, and providing insights to help informed decision-marking. Creating and delivering tailored change management plans and required supporting plans such as Communications and Engagement Plans. Building and maintaining trusted relationships with key stakeholders, which includes providing guidance, addressing their needs and concerns, and assisting leaders to be the ‘face of change’ through practical and strategic change management techniques. Leading, delivering, and monitoring engagements to ensure regular, meaningful opportunities for stakeholders and impacted groups that enable buy-in, builds understanding, and support early adoption. Please refer to the attached position description, for a comprehensive list of roles and responsibilities.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through the strategic drive of Students First . This enabling role will play a pivotal role in the successful development and launch of the Flinders HealthCare Centre; a centre designed to offer modern, simulation training facilities that enhance student workforce readiness and strengthen their impact when entering communities as practitioners.   About You You have highly developed interpersonal, and relationship management skills, with experience building and maintaining effective relationships with stakeholders and supporting individuals and groups through diverse types and levels of change. You bring a proven ability to lead and facilitate stakeholder engagement at all levels, develop high-quality communications, and oversee and deliver learning, readiness and adoption activities. You have excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to communicate clearly and effectively with diverse audiences, and extensive experience fostering flexible ways of working to drive collaboration, adaptability and successful outcomes. You will also be able to demonstrate: Postgraduate qualifications and extensive relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of experience and/or education and/or training. Experience in leading and delivering successful change management programs and services across entire project lifecycles. Extensive experience in overseeing and undertaking change impact analysis and creating fit-for-purpose change management plans for impacted groups. Experience in leading and overseeing project and change management resources to deliver change management activities.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include:   Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. SA: Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the Position Description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Dr Mailys Stirling Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 01 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Research-Assistant_JR0000017782": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Part-time or Full-time Position (0.6FTE-1FTE) | Fixed Term until end of June 2027. Higher Education Officer Level 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description   About the Role   Join the National Centre for Education and Training on Addiction (NCETA) Research Team and contribute to the impactful research led by the internationally recognised Centre that works as a catalyst for change in the alcohol and other drugs field. Working closely as part of the wider Research Team, the Centre Director and government stakeholders, you will have the opportunity to support a variety of research activities imbedded in diverse projects. If you enjoy working collaboratively as part of a team on research and project-based tasks, we want to hear from you. Take a closer look at some of the everyday tasks you will have the opportunity to work on below: Assisting in capturing and sharing project outcomes and organisational Key Performance Indicators. Assisting with research ethics applications, grant applications and literature reviews. Maintaining spreadsheets, databases, EndNote libraries and maintaining accurate records of research procedures. Assisting with qualitative and quantitative data management, including data cleaning, coding, analysis, and summaries of findings.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters , by 2035, through directly contributing to the Strategic Driver of Research that Matters . This enabling role works to support innovative research with a goal of reducing the harms from alcohol and other drugs along the continuum from prevention through to treatment, to improve health outcomes for communities locally and globally.   About You   You are a proactive and detail-oriented research professional with a strong foundation in public health, health science or psychology, coupled with a passion for contributing to meaningful health research. Comfortable working both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team, you bring excellent organisational skills, research rigour and a collaborative approach to supporting research projects from data collection through to reporting and communications.   To be successful in this position, you will also bring: Completion of degree in relevant area (e.g. psychology, public health or science) or an equivalent combination of experience and/or education and/or training. Demonstrated well-developed writing and communication skills, particularly related to assisting in scientific manuscript preparation, industry reports, grant writing, or research dissemination (e.g. via newsletters, websites, social media). Well-developed qualitative and quantitative research skills. Well-developed computer skills including Microsoft Office products such as Word, Excel, and PowerPoint, statistical software (e.g., SPSS, R) and Endnote.   College of Medicine and Public Health             At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document. This should be no more than 3 pages, and address each of the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged, and can, as far as practicably possible, participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, including being accompanied by an accredited assistance animal with public access rights as per the Disability Policy , please advise the contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Sarah Powell. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 01 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Charles-Darwin-University-Darwin--Larrakia-Country/Operational-Support-Officer--Rural-and-Remote-Health_JR0000017372": {
      "title": "Operational Support Officer, Rural and Remote Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full time | Fixed term until 31 December 2027 Professional HEO5 | $84,599 - $93,366 Location: Darwin / Larrakia View Position Description   About the Role   We are seeking a proactive and highly organised Operational Support Officer to join the Operational Support team and deliver high quality operational and administrative support to the Discipline of Rural and Remote Health (RRH) at Flinders University. In this diverse role, you will be responsible for coordinating operational activities including conferences and events, stakeholder visits, while providing executive support to leadership staff. Based at the Darwin Casuarina campus, this position contributes to the ongoing improvements of operational procedures, administrative systems and work instructions to support consistent and effective service delivery across the discipline.   Key responsibilities include: Providing operational and administrative support, including calendar management, coordination of meetings and conferences, and the preparation and distribution of correspondence. Building and maintaining effective working relationships with leadership staff and key stakeholders. Supporting the development and ongoing maintenance of operational procedures, processes and templates to support efficient and consistent service delivery. Coordinating travel arrangements for staff, students, visitors and groups, ensuring compliance with university policies and procedures.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan, Changing Lives, Every One Matters , by 2035 through being collaborative, responsive and service-focused, supporting the University's commitment to the Core Enabler of Stronger Together and ensuring effective engagement with staff, students and stakeholders.   About You   You are a highly organised and proactive professional with experience providing operational and administrative support in a complex environment. With strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills, you thrive in a fast-paced setting, effectively managing competing priorities while delivering high-quality customer service and operational outcomes. You are solutions-focused, detail-oriented and committed to continuous improvement, with the ability to work both independently and collaboratively to support organisational goals.   To be successful in this position, you will demonstrate: Proven experience providing operational and administrative support, including coordinating events, travel, facilities, resources and business operations within a complex organisation. Strong organisational and problem-solving skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and maintain a high level of accuracy and attention to detail. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build positive relationships and work collaboratively with a diverse range of internal and external stakeholders. Sound knowledge of contemporary administrative systems, software and processes, with the ability to interpret and apply policies and procedures to support efficient service delivery and continuous improvement.   Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT   At Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT , our aim is to be a hub for innovative health research, education, and workforce development across the Northern Territory.  We engage with students, community, and stakeholders to provide critical programs, such as the Northern Territory Medical Program, Paramedicine, Rural Clinical School, and the University Department of Rural Health.   Our postgraduate education pathways (Remote Health Practice program), provides workforce development through Indigenous health and clinical skills training, and we have a community driven research program led by researchers committed to improving health outcomes. We work alongside Poche SA+NT focussing on Indigenous leadership capacity development and developing the Indigenous health workforce.  All activities are driven by our values which focus on the long-term and short-term needs of the NT community.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place. We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   undefined Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are required to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the NT Health policy. Northern Territory Government legislation (the Care and Protection of Children Act 2007) now requires all individuals in child related employment or whose duties involve, or are likely to involve, contact with a child, to hold a valid 'Working with Children Clearance' (Ochre Card). This position involves child related work and is assessed as requiring a Working with Children Clearance/Ochre Card. undefined How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . Candidates who have already applied for this position are not required to submit a new application. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Amy Schulz. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 28 Oct 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Lecturer-in-Critical-Indigenous-Studies--Identified-_JR0000017765": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Critical Indigenous Studies (Identified)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Teaching Specialist Continuing position Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 pa Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description Acknowledgement of Country: Flinders University acknowledges the Traditional Owners and Custodians of the lands on which its campuses are located. These are the Traditional Lands of the Arrernte, Dagoman, First Nations of the South East, First Peoples of the River Murray & Mallee region, Jawoyn, Kaurna, Larrakia, Ngadjuri, Ngarrindjeri, Ramindjeri, Warumungu, Wardaman and Yolngu people. We honour their Elders past, present and emerging. About the Role We are seeking a dedicated and forward-thinking Lecturer (Teaching Specialist) to contribute to student success and excellence in teaching and learning across undergraduate and postgraduate programs. With growing student enrolments and increasing demand across the Critical Indigenous Studies teaching portfolio, this position offers an opportunity to play a vital role in sustaining and strengthening the discipline and its interdisciplinary engagement across the University. You will contribute to high-quality teaching, curriculum development, topic coordination, assessment and student support, helping ensure positive student outcomes and an interdisciplinary and culturally responsive learning environment. This position will be predominantly concerned with teaching and teaching-related duties, with responsibilities including, but not limited to: Direct teaching informed by approaches that enhance the student experience; Curriculum design and review; Making an independent contribution to the review, renewal, development and design of curricula and courses that are attractive to existing and new cohorts of students and that nurture creativity and critical thinking skills; Providing support, academic counselling and professional advice to students concerning their studies, professional development and career opportunities. Working collaboratively across disciplines to deliver interdisciplinary learning experiences that enhance student engagement and success. The filling of this position is intended to constitute a special/equal opportunity measure under section 56 of the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (SA) and Anti-Discrimination Act 1992 under section 35 (NT). The position is therefore only open to Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander people. The successful candidate will be required to provide Confirmation of Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander descent   About You We are seeking an engaging and collaborative educator with a strong commitment to Critical Indigenous Studies and the delivery of culturally responsive, academically rigorous teaching. You must bring a student-centred approach to learning and a commitment to creating inclusive educational experiences that support student achievement, belonging and success. The successful candidate will demonstrate the following, among other capabilities: Experience of significant teaching and learning experience and excellence. Evidence of use of learning and teaching approaches which enhance student success. Evidence of successful and independent innovation in learning and teaching delivery or design, including use of e-learning technologies. About the College/Portfolio Through our world-class teaching and research, we equip students with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and advance human development. The College is dedicated to cultivating bright minds and inspiring innovative, passionate, and highly skilled graduates for future ready careers, and delivering connections for life.    Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.  So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.  Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy    Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include:  Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. In addition, it is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working With Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA).  How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . For more information regarding this position, please contact Azra Coombe .   Applications to be submitted before 10:00 pm (ACDT) on Sunday, 1 November 2026. Please note, late applications and applications sent via agencies will not be accepted . At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 01 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Programs-and-Events-Officer_JR0000017890": {
      "title": "Programs and Events Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nFixed-term, full-time until 31 Dec 2027. Higher Education Officer Level 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role FUSA is seeking a motivated Programs and Events Officer to foster a vibrant community across Flinders Campuses, through the development of programs, events, and activities. Supporting the Senior Coordinator Programming and Communications, you will engage with diverse student groups and individuals to assess needs and co-create solutions that build community and are aimed at improving student experience. You will also be responsible for:  Working closely with other University stakeholders, including student leaders to contribute to the delivery of the social component of a University wide orientation and welcome program. Building relationships with a diverse range of student groups including students from marginalised communities, students with additional needs and student leaders. Engaging in best practice event and project management, including the development and implementation of mechanisms for the ongoing review and effective evaluation of events against key performance indicators. About You You will be an energetic self-starter and excellent communicator who can build positive relationships with a diverse range of students. You will also be a community orientated person, who can create events in partnership with diverse student groups. The successful Programs and Events Officer will also demonstrate: Experience in supporting the development, coordination and delivery of a program of engagement activities, events and activations aligned to placemaking and/or building a vibrant campus culture. Sound knowledge and skills in event and program planning and the delivery processes and techniques employed to achieve required objectives within scope and budget. Tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline with subsequent relevant work experience or an equivalent combination of experience and/or education and/or training About Flinders University Student Association (FUSA) Flinders University Student Association (FUSA) is the student organisation that represents the rights and interests of students at Flinders University. FUSA is responsible for community building events on campus, including Orientation.  FUSA also provides a wide range of other services including managing clubs and associations, provides welfare and advocacy services. Life at Flinders    They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.  Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.  Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.  Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.  This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit Suitability Statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. For more information regarding this position, please contact Caleb Osborne Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 10 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/IDS-Platforms-Analyst---Coordinator_JR0000017879": {
      "title": "IDS Platforms Analyst & Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Continuing  Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $94,456 - $100,710  Location:  Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description   About the Role   The Platforms Analyst & Coordinator works under general to broad direction, and is responsible for contributing to analysis, support and coordination of activities related to the ongoing operation and development of the relevant platforms and applications.    As this is a customer facing role, operating on a diverse range of research specific SAS platforms, you will be responsible for; contributing to requirements analysis activities, interpreting business requirements and translating them into technical requirements - along with supporting business change impact analysis to identify appropriate change management activities to support system changes.   Some of the key position responsibilities are: Supporting the technical change, configuration and release management process for the relevant platforms and applications in alignment with IDS frameworks, principles and standards. Assisting as required with both formal and informal market scanning and benchmarking activities to be aware of emerging market offerings and trends. Contributing to the continuous development of relevant platforms and applications by analysing, configuring, testing and documenting new functionalities and capabilities. Providing timely and effective advice and support to customers and stakeholders by identifying improvements to support services. undefined. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to “Changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters”, by 2035 through supporting the stable, reliable and performant operations, assisting our researchers to smoothly access relevant platforms and applications. By working collaboratively across teams and embracing agile ways of working, the role helps deliver efficient, high-quality outcomes and a commitment to excellence and innovation.   About You  The role ideally will suit an early career focused individual with excellent customer service skill. You are committed to continuous improvement and enjoy working in a small, agile team.   While you work independently, you also follow established procedures and have a curious nature.  You will have previous experience in agile ways of working to manage, refine, and prioritise an activity backlog based on relevant customer and organisational priorities, and well-developed, planning, analytical, and critical thinking skills.   See the “How to apply and Information” section at the end of this ad for more about what you will bring to the role.  The ideal candidate will hold a qualification in Information Technology or relevant technical area, and / or equivalent relevant experience in an IT environment.  About Information and Digital Services    Information and Digital Services (IDS) are a pivotal resource of the University, providing information and digital technology services and support to the Flinders University staff and student community. The IDS team apply technical expertise to support university projects and provide core services such as the IDS Service Desk, Audio visual, simulation and event services, Information security and identity access management services and more.    Life at Flinders     They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.    So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice      Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment     Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.    Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan     Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy       Prescribed Conditions for Employment     Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.  This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University.  The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.   How to Apply and Information    You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 2 pages, and address the below Key Position Capabilities of the  position description: Well-developed thinking, planning and analytical skills Well-developed interpersonal influence and demonstrated ability to negotiate and communicate effectively with staff and customers across a diverse organisation particularly during the design, management and implementation of customer solutions. Experience supporting and maintaining Commercial-Off-The-Shelf (COTS) applications and Software as a Service and enterprise applications and systems. General knowledge and experience in the design and execution of application upgrades including planning, test plan, execution and program close out using good change management principles and  practices. General knowledge of the principles and practices of the software development lifecycle. For more information regarding this position, please contact Scott Anderson Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through  recruitment@flinders.edu.au  or call 08 8201 2383 Certification National Police Certificate - Australian Federal Police Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 09 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Flinders-Rural-Health-SA-Murray-Bridge--Ngarrindjeri-Country/Director--Regional-Training-Hub-SA_JR0000017364-1": {
      "title": "Director, Regional Training Hub SA",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPosition Details Fixed Term until 31 December 2027 | Part-Time at 0.8FTE Academic Level D | $169,706 - $186,706 p.a. (pro-rata) Location: Any RRH SA Campus View Position Description About the Role We are seeking an experienced and influential medical educator and clinician to join Flinders University as an Associate Professor and Director, Regional Training Hub (RTH) SA. This leadership role is responsible for driving excellence in rural medical education, clinical teaching and workforce development across South Australia. Working collaboratively with academic staff, health services, government and industry stakeholders, you will contribute to the clinical years in the MD program, strengthen rural training pathways, support the next generation of medical professionals, and contribute to improved health workforce outcomes in regional and remote communities. Key responsibilities include: Providing strategic leadership for the South Australian Regional Training Hub and the SA Rural Medical Program, ensuring high-quality clinical education, teaching excellence and continuous improvement. Building and maintaining strong partnerships with health services, professional bodies and government stakeholders to develop and strengthen rural medical training pathways and workforce initiatives. Leading and supporting academic staff while delivering innovative, student-centred learning experiences, curriculum development and clinical teaching across the medical program. Mentoring and supporting medical students and junior doctors, promoting rural and remote career pathways through career planning, professional development and placement opportunities. This role is well suited to a local clinician with teaching or academic experience who is looking to further develop their career in higher education, bringing their clinical expertise into a meaningful teaching and academic environment. This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035, through directly contributing to the strategic drivers of Student Centricity and Industry & Enterprise Partnerships, strengthening rural medical education and workforce pathways across South Australia. About You You are a respected clinician and accomplished medical educator with a passion for rural and remote health training. Bringing extensive clinical experience and a strong track record of educational leadership, you thrive in collaborative environments and are committed to developing future medical professionals. You are skilled at building meaningful partnerships, leading high-performing teams, and driving innovation in teaching, curriculum development and workforce initiatives that strengthen health outcomes for regional communities. To be successful in this position, you will demonstrate: Extensive clinical experience and current registration, or eligibility for registration, with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (AHPRA), supported by a doctorate or equivalent professional standing. A proven record of excellence in tertiary and clinical education, including leadership in the design, delivery and evaluation of innovative teaching and learning practices. Success leading and mentoring academic teams, managing teaching programs, and contributing to curriculum development, quality assurance and accreditation activities. Exceptional communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to collaborate effectively with students, colleagues, health services, industry partners and community stakeholders. Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA (FRRHSA) facilitates high quality multidisciplinary health education and research in our State's regional areas. We have a strong national and international reputation for providing community-engaged inter-professional training for medical, nursing, and allied health students. This includes the SA Rural Medical (SARM) program and multidisciplinary rural clinical placements. Through this practice, we bring new and exciting opportunities to actively engage and work with rural communities.  Life at Flinders They say know your place. We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy. It is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA). A valid South Australian Driver’s License is an essential requirement of this position and must be maintained for the duration of employment. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Claire Drummond . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 15 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Vice-President-and-Executive-Dean_JR0000017864": {
      "title": "Vice-President and Executive Dean, College of Medicine and Public Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Fixed-Term (5 years) Senior Manager Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the opportunity Flinders University is seeking to appoint an outstanding academic leader as its next Vice-President and Executive Dean, College of Medicine and Public Health. This is a significant opportunity to lead one of the University’s largest Colleges, building on a strong record of achievement in education and research while shaping its next phase of growth and development. Reporting to the Vice-Chancellor and serving as a member of the University’s Senior Executive Team, the Vice-President and Executive Dean will provide strategic and academic leadership across the College, advancing research excellence, educational quality and external engagement. A particular priority will be to build on the College’s strong research performance, developing academic capability, strengthening partnerships and driving further growth in research activity and impact. The College has a distinctive national profile, supported by significant investment in health and medical research infrastructure, close partnerships with health services and an extensive medical education footprint across regional and remote Australia, including the Northern Territory. Further opportunities arise through the development of the Flinders HealthCARE Centre and its ambitions for interdisciplinary health education. The successful candidate will play a leading role in realising these opportunities, fostering collaboration across the University and contributing to the delivery of Agenda 2035: Impact. With Purpose. About you You will be a distinguished academic and research leader with a PhD or equivalent qualification, an outstanding record of research achievement and demonstrated success in building and leading high-performing academic environments. You will bring the credibility to engage with the medical and research communities, alongside the strategic, financial and organisational leadership capabilities required to lead a large and complex College. Ambitious, collaborative and collegial, you will have a demonstrated ability to develop others, build productive relationships with clinical, government and research partners, and work effectively across disciplinary boundaries. Importantly, you will bring an institution-wide perspective, with the capacity to contribute to Flinders’ broader ambitions while inspiring the College to achieve even greater success. Life at Flinders Flinders University is entering an exciting period of growth and investment, guided by its Agenda 2035 ambition to change 10 million lives for the better. With a strong commitment to student success, research that matters and meaningful partnerships with industry and communities, Flinders offers an environment in which ambitious leaders can make a lasting contribution. Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide, Flinders offers flexible working arrangements, extensive professional development and a vibrant campus life in Adelaide, one of the world’s most liveable cities. Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. We strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. How to Apply Odgers is assisting Flinders University with this appointment. More information on the role can be found at www.odgers.com/96913 . For further information and a confidential conversation please contact Dr Alistair Clark on Alistair.Clark@odgers.com or Mazin Almaimani on +61 3 8375 7408 or Mazin.Almaimani@odgers.com .  Downloadable Content Candidate Brief Published_Flinders University_VP - Executive Dean, College of Medicine - Public Health.pdf Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 23 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Governance---Policy-Support-Office_JR0000017861": {
      "title": "Governance & Policy Support Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nContinuing | Fixed term (12 months) Higher Education Officer Level 7 | $102,589 - $113,561 p.a. Location: Bedford Park View Position Description About the Role Under broad direction, the Governance & Policy Support Officer supports the effective operation of the University Council, Academic Senate and their subcommittees, by coordinating & hosting meetings, taking minutes, preparing governance documentation and maintaining accurate records and compliance information. The role ensures smooth governance processes through clear communication, efficient logistics and proactive stakeholder support. The role ensures smooth governance processes through clear communication, efficient logistics and proactive stakeholder support, along with maintaining the University’s policy and delegation’s library, assisting policy owners, ensuring governance documents remain current and consistent, and contributes to other important governance and policy projects. The role will also handle Freedom of Information enquiries (following training being provided). Key responsibilities include: Supporting the General Counsel & University Secretary to ensure the effective and efficient operation of Council, Academic Senate and their sub-committees Supporting the Senior Policy and Projects Officer to ensure the effective operation of the University’s policy framework Maintaining accurate records, registers and compliance information relating to the University’s external entities About You This is a terrific opportunity for someone who is interested in learning and enjoys interacting with the decision makers (executive and board) within an organisation. The role ensures smooth governance processes through clear communication, efficient logistics and proactive stakeholder support, so you will bring high level interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills, along with the ability to negotiate and liaise with a broad range of stakeholders. In addition, you should also demonstrate: Experience in, and understanding of, governance practices and principles. Proven ability to prepare complex correspondence, papers, briefings, minutes and reports with a high degree of accuracy and attention to detail Competency in using a range of computer applications, including word-processing, Excel, Teams and Zoom, and accessing information from websites, spreadsheets and database It is expected that the successful candidate will have completed a degree and have subsequent relevant experience; or possess an equivalent combination of experience and/or education and/or training. Even if you don’t have all the listed experience, we encourage you to apply if you feel this role aligns with your strengths About Governance, Legal and Risk Governance, Legal and Risk provide governance, legal, policy, compliance, insurance and risk management support to the University and its staff. The Governance, Legal and Risk team provides advice on university governance and decision-making processes and plays a pivotal role in ensuring that Flinders has policies and processes in place to protect its people, assets, and activities. Life at Flinders      They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and change makers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.    Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.    Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.     So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice      Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.    Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan    Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment A current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check which is satisfactory to the University will be required by Flinders University before the successful applicant can commence in this position . A criminal record will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.     How to Apply and Information You are required to submit cover letter detailing your suitability as per the position description. For more information regarding this position, please contact Marc Davies Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 03 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Integration-Specialist_JR0000017855": {
      "title": "Senior Integration Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Continuing  Higher Education Officer Level 8 | $116,349 - $128,806 Location:  Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description   About the Role   The Senior Integration Specialist is the hands-on technical lead for integration services within IDS. Working under broad direction as part of the IDS integration capability hub, you will design, build, test, implement and support integration solutions in accordance with IDS integration architecture principles, guardrails and design standards. This role is focused on technical delivery and operational reliability. Integration architecture strategy and roadmap ownership sit with our Integration and Solution Architects, and you will work closely with them to turn that direction into secure, dependable solutions that you are accountable for delivering and supporting. Your key responsibilities will include:   Integration delivery and lifecycle: Design, build, test, deploy and maintain secure, scalable integrations on Boomi or equivalent, managing change, release, version control and upgrades across the full lifecycle. Operational reliability: Own production integration services, monitoring performance, resolving incidents and root causes, and ensuring integrations meet agreed service levels. Security, standards and assurance: Build security, privacy and audit requirements into every solution, and conduct design reviews that validate compliance with IDS integration principles, guardrails and standards. Capability and continuous improvement: Maintain reusable patterns, API standards and technical documentation, and use service metrics to improve the reliability, supportability and cost-effectiveness of integration services. Collaboration and technical leadership: Work with Integration and Solution Architects, product owners, project teams and vendors, providing design guidance and mentoring to promote consistent engineering practice.   By delivering dependable integrations that connect systems and data across Flinders, the role underpins digital transformation and helps ensure technology is implemented in an ethical, sustainable and agile way.   About You   You are a hands-on integration professional who enjoys solving complex technical problems and takes pride in building solutions that are secure, well documented and easy to support. In this role you will own technical outcomes, ensure production reliability, exercise sound technical judgement and provide technical leadership across the integration services you deliver.   Your analytical skills are matched by strong written communication, including the ability to produce quality design documents, interface specifications, work instructions and change requests. You build strong working relationships and communicate clearly with technical and non-technical audiences, using your influence and negotiation skills to guide stakeholders, project teams and vendors through the design, delivery and support of integration solutions.   You also possess:   Extensive hands-on experience delivering application and data integration solutions across the full lifecycle, from requirements and design through testing, deployment and transition to support. Proven expertise developing, configuring and supporting an enterprise integration platform (such as Boomi or equivalent), with strong knowledge of API design, integration patterns, data mapping and error handling. Demonstrated ownership of production integration services, ensuring they are reliable, scalable and maintainable through incident resolution, root cause analysis, performance optimisation and operational reporting. A sound understanding of integration security, privacy, access control and audit requirements, and the ability to undertake technical design reviews that assess compliance with architecture principles, guardrails and standards, and recommend corrective actions. Experience with integration testing, release management and change control, and with improving capability through reusable patterns, automation, documentation and continuous improvement. Demonstrated technical leadership through design guidance, knowledge sharing and mentoring, and experience delivering in agile, scaled agile or matrix environments.   The ideal candidate will also hold an appropriate tertiary qualification in Information Technology or a relevant technical area, and / or equivalent relevant experience in an IT environment. Experience in the higher education sector will be highly regarded.   About Information and Digital Services     Information and Digital Services (IDS) are a pivotal resource of the University, providing information and digital technology services and support to the Flinders University staff and student community. The IDS team apply technical expertise to support university projects and provide core services such as the IDS Service Desk, Audio visual, simulation and event services, Information security and identity access management services and more.     Life at Flinders      They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.    Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.    Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.     So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice       Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment      Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.     Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan     Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy        Prescribed Conditions for Employment      Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.     This includes a current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and a Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks.  A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.     How to Apply and Information     You are required to submit a CV, along with a Cover Letter as a separate document, telling us how your experiences have set you up for success, and why you are the right person for the job. For more information regarding this position, please contact  James Reed Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through  recruitment@flinders.edu.au  or call 08 8201 2383.  Certification National Police Check - Australian Federal Police Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 05 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
