    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Sustainability-Coordinator_JR0000017834": {
      "title": "Sustainability Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Continuing Professional HEO6 | $94,456 - $100,710 Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description   Flinders University is bringing its 2030 Sustainability Strategy to life through practical action across climate, biodiversity, resource efficiency, sustainable campus operations and community engagement. We are seeking a collaborative and proactive Sustainability Coordinator to help deliver a diverse range of projects and initiatives that embed sustainability across the University.   Based at Bedford Park, but working across our campuses, you'll connect with staff, students, academics and external partners to translate sustainability ambitions into meaningful outcomes. From engaging our community and supporting environmental initiatives to coordinating projects and improving everyday practices, this is a varied role where your contribution will make a tangible difference. We are the first institution in the world to achieve a coveted Gold rating under the newly released version 3 of the international Sustainability Tracking, Assessment & Rating System. To learn more about this award and our sustainability initiatives, head over to our website: Sustainability at Flinders About the role Reporting to the Head of Sustainability, you'll coordinate a diverse range of initiatives supporting Flinders University's 2030 Sustainability Strategy, working collaboratively across academic and professional areas. You'll play an important role in connecting people, coordinating practical projects, communicating sustainability achievements and supporting the effective operation of the Sustainability team. Your work will contribute to a wide range of projects and outcomes, including: Deliver sustainability projects: Coordinate initiatives across climate action, biodiversity, resource recovery, sustainable food systems, water efficiency, active transport and sustainable procurement. Engage and inspire our community: Deliver staff and student engagement programs, orientation activities, Sustainability Week, workshops, volunteering opportunities and behaviour change campaigns. Communicate sustainability achievements: Develop newsletters, digital content, educational resources and communications that promote sustainability initiatives, research and positive outcomes. Support climate resilience and biodiversity: Coordinate working groups, monitoring activities, stakeholder workshops and practical initiatives that enhance campus sustainability and climate adaptation. Connect people and partnerships: Work with academic staff, students and external organisations to support sustainability-related teaching, research, experiential learning and community projects. Support sustainability governance and operations: Coordinate meetings, project documentation, reporting and administrative activities, including procurement, budgets and records management For a more comprehensive list of Key Position Responsibilities, please view the Position Description About You You're a proactive, organised and collaborative sustainability professional who enjoys translating ideas into practical action and bringing people together to achieve positive outcomes. You have strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills, an adaptable approach and the ability to coordinate multiple activities across a diverse organisation. You're equally comfortable delivering a project, facilitating a workshop, developing communications or supporting the day-to-day operations of a busy team. You will bring: Experience coordinating sustainability, environmental or organisational improvement projects, including managing activities, budgets, timelines and deliverables. A sound understanding of contemporary sustainability principles and practices, including climate action, biodiversity, resource efficiency and sustainable operations. Strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills, with the ability to engage effectively with diverse internal and external stakeholders. Experience coordinating communications, engagement activities, workshops, events or behaviour change initiatives. Well-developed written communication skills, including preparing reports, project documentation, digital communications and educational resources. Strong organisational and administrative skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and support procurement, financial and operational processes. Experience implementing sustainability initiatives within a university, public sector or similarly complex organisation will be highly regarded. The ideal candidate will hold a degree in sustainability, environmental management, environmental science, communications or a related discipline, together with relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of experience, education and training   Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.     Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.     Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.     Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.    So,  why work at Flinders?    17% Superannuation + salary packaging options    Flexible working arrangements    Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program   Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers     We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.    Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).    Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice       Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. This includes a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.      How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description For more information regarding this position, please contact William Van Ausdal Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 04 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Research-Assistant_JR0000017841": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nPosition Details Fixed-Term (12 months) | Part Time (0.5FTE) Higher Education Officer Level 5 | $84,599 - $93,366 (pro rata) Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description   About the Role Join the Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute and contribute to the CRESCENT Study, an exciting research project investigating how light sensitivity and light exposure patterns may influence the development of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) following traumatic injury. This is a unique opportunity to work closely with the Trauma Service Team at Flinders Medical Centre and provide hands-on support to the Postdoctoral Research Associate and Chief Investigator in the delivery of this important research. Working directly with participants throughout their research journey, you will play a key role in recruitment, assessments and follow-up activities that contribute to a better understanding of trauma, PTSD and recovery.   This position is offered at 0.5 FTE, with flexibility required across multiple weekdays to support participant scheduling and the operational needs of the study.   As a Research Assistant, you will be responsible for: Supporting participant identification, recruitment and enrolment for the CRESCENT Study. Conducting research interviews, assessments and participant follow-up activities. Coordinating research activities between participants, hospital staff and the research team. Maintaining accurate study records, research data and documentation in line with approved protocols.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through advancing Research that Matters. The role supports research that aims to improve our understanding of trauma, PTSD and recovery, ultimately contributing to better health outcomes for individuals and communities.   About You   You are a compassionate and organised research professional who enjoys working directly with people and making a meaningful contribution to health research. Comfortable building rapport in sensitive situations, you bring empathy, professionalism and a trauma-informed approach to participant interactions. You are confident working both independently and collaboratively and are motivated by the opportunity to support research that has a genuine impact on people's lives and wellbeing.   To thrive in this role, you will bring: A degree in psychology, health sciences, medicine, social sciences, public health or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of experience and training. Demonstrated experience working with people in professional settings, particularly where conversations may involve sensitive or potentially distressing experiences. The ability to build strong relationships through effective communication, interpersonal skills and a collaborative approach. Excellent organisational skills, attention to detail, and the ability to manage competing priorities while working independently. Experience within hospital, trauma, mental health or related clinical settings, and/or a demonstrated interest in trauma-informed research and practice will be highly regarded.   College of Medicine and Public Health      At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do.   Life at Flinders   They say know your place. We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Ranked in the top 2% of universities worldwide.   Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Dayna Easton Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 05 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Content-Producer--Alumni---Advancement_JR0000017840": {
      "title": "Content Producer, Alumni & Advancement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Fixed Term (2 years) Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $94,456 - $100,710 Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role When we tell you this job will make a difference, we mean it! This newly created fixed-term position plays a key role in supporting the development and delivery of engaging content that strengthens relationships with donors, alumni and supporters while showcasing the impact of philanthropy at Flinders University. Working across both Alumni and Advancement portfolios, you'll contribute to communications that support the University's ambitious vision and help bring major initiatives, including the Flinders HealthCARE Centre project, to life. Reporting to the Marketing & Communications Partner, Advancement, you'll create compelling stories and multimedia content that inspire support, build engagement and celebrate the achievements of our alumni and donor communities. You'll have the opportunity to produce content across a diverse range of channels, including web, social media, email campaigns, publications, photography and video, while helping ensure our digital presence remains current, accessible and aligned with best-practice standards Your Key Position Responsibilities include: Developing and delivering engaging content across digital and print channels, including websites, social media, EDMs, newsletters, publications and campaign materials. Writing, editing and proof-reading content tailored to a range of audiences, including alumni, donors, community partners and stakeholders. Producing dynamic multimedia content, including photography, video and digital assets that support Alumni and Advancement initiatives. Maintaining and updating website content in line with brand, accessibility, UX/UI and SEO best practice.   For a comprehensive list of Key Position Responsibilities, please view the Position Description     About You You are a creative and versatile content specialist who enjoys transforming stories into engaging communications that connect with audiences and achieve meaningful outcomes. With experience creating content across multiple channels, you combine exceptional writing and editing skills with a strong understanding of digital communications, audience engagement and multimedia production. You are highly organised, collaborative and able to manage competing priorities while maintaining exceptional attention to detail. To be successful in this role, you will demonstrate: A talent for emotive storytelling that grabs readers’ attention and answers the question “why does this matter?” Strong experience writing, editing and proof-reading content for online and print audiences. Experience creating multimedia content, including photography and video. Proficiency in creative production tools such as Adobe Photoshop, Acrobat and Canva. Experience using content management systems (preferably AEM), email marketing platforms (preferably Campaign Monitor) and SEO principles. Strong stakeholder engagement skills and the ability to work collaboratively across diverse teams.   Experience working within a higher education, membership, community or not-for-profit environment will be highly regarded. About the Office of Alumni and Advancement   The Alumni and Advancement team exists to foster enduring relationships with alumni, community members, donors, and stakeholders, thereby enhancing Flinders University’s impact locally and globally. Through a whole-of-university approach, we champion a vibrant culture of philanthropy and engagement, securing vital financial support for students and pioneering research and adding value to our graduates’ lives at every stage of life. In alignment with the University’s overarching vision, the Alumni and Advancement team is dedicated to building a connected, supportive, and influential Flinders community.   Life at Flinders   We're transforming and investing in people and facilities to create contemporary, stimulating, and satisfying learning and work environments that reflect our core values of excellence, innovation, courage, and integrity. Flinders is refocusing its strategic priorities with the aim of elevating its performance to be a top ten Australian university, and amongst the top 1% in the world.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,   why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment   Flinders University is proudly committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy and we strive to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Flinders University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive environment, free from gender‑based violence. Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre‑employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   This includes a Gender‑Based Violence declaration, in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender‑Based Violence (2025), and a Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check. A criminal record will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.   undefined   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, a cover letter including a proactive statement about why you are suitable for the role and a Suitability Statement as a separate document, which addresses the below Key Position Capabilities of the position description : Demonstrated experience in donor relations, stewardship or a related field within a higher education or non-profit environment. Exemplary written communication skills including experience in writing, editing and proof-reading content for online and print channels tailored to diverse audiences. Proven experience in Adobe Photoshop, Acrobat and InDesign for creative production & experience in photography and video production Experience in Content Management Systems (preferably AEM), email management (preferably Campaign Monitor) and SEO to improve reach. General knowledge of industry best practice principles and standards for digital content across web, social and email marketing. Including the ability to interpret and apply relevant policies and procedures, including content delivery standards, usability and accessibility. For more information regarding this position, please contact Heidi Warren Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged, and are able to, as far as practicably possible, participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, including being accompanied by an accredited assistance animal with public access rights as per the Disability Policy , please advise the contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 04 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Lecturer-in-Women-s-and-Gender-Studies_JR0000017832": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Women's and Gender Studies",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full Time | Continuing appointment Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 pa | 17% Super View Position Description Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country About the Role: We are seeking an innovative and collaborative Lecturer in Women’s and Gender Studies to contribute to high-quality teaching, research, and engagement across the College of Human Sciences and Culture. This is an exciting opportunity for an academic who is committed to interdisciplinary scholarship, contemporary teaching practices, and preparing students to make a positive difference in society. In this role, you will deliver engaging undergraduate and/or postgraduate teaching across the Women’s and Gender Studies suite, developing innovative learning materials and applying contemporary, student-centred teaching approaches across face-to-face and online environments. You will contribute to a vibrant research culture through independent, high-quality research and/or creative activity, including scholarly publications, external research funding, and research student supervision. For a full list of the key position responsibilities, please refer to the position description . This position is open to those with Australian working rights. Relocation support will be available. About You To be successful in this role, you will have completed a Phd, EdD or an equivalent qualification. The successful candidate will also require: Demonstrated evidence of, or capacity to, undertake high-quality teaching in a tertiary environment, including involvement in topic coordination and curriculum design. Evidence of successful and independent innovation in learning and teaching delivery or design, including use of e-learning technologies. Evidence of a student-centred approach. Demonstrated experience or capability to engage with students (and deliver teaching modules) via online learning platforms.   For a full list of the key position capabilities of the successful candidate, please refer to the position description .   College of Human Sciences and Culture   Through our world-class teaching and research, we equip students with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and advance human development. The College is dedicated to cultivating bright minds and inspiring innovative, passionate, and highly skilled graduates for future ready careers, and delivering connections for life.  Life at Flinders Flinders University is driven by a bold purpose: to change lives and change the world. Through Agenda 2035 – Impact. With Purpose. , we are expanding the scale, reach, and ambition of our research to deliver meaningful outcomes for communities, industry, and global health systems. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Wide range of professional development activities and services We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia). Support future initiatives like the Flinders HealthCARE Centre Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.    In addition, it is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working With Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA).    How to Apply and Information As well as a CV, you are required to submit a Suitability Statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . For more information regarding this position, please contact Prof Kate Douglas . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 04 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Associate-Lecturer---Lecturer-in-Indigenous-Knowledges-and-Culture_JR0000017827": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer / Lecturer in Indigenous Knowledges and Culture",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time Position (36.75 hours per week) | Fixed Term until end of December 2027 Academic Level A | $90,930 - $110,197 p.a. + 17% superannuation Academic Level B | $115,871 - $137,122 p.a. + 17% superannuation Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Level A Position Description View Level B Position Description   Acknowledgement of Country:    Flinders University acknowledges the Traditional Owners and Custodians of the lands on which its campuses are located. These are the Traditional Lands of the Arrernte, Dagoman, First Nations of the South East, First Peoples of the River Murray & Mallee region, Jawoyn, Kaurna, Larrakia, Ngadjuri, Ngarrindjeri, Ramindjeri, Warumungu, Wardaman and Yolngu people.    We honour their Elders past, present and emerging.    The successful candidate will be appointed to either the Level A or Level B position. Please indicate on your application which position(s) you would like to be considered for. About the Positions: We are seeking a vibrant and collaborative Associate Lecturer / Lecturer in Indigenous Knowledges and Culture to join our growing Aboriginal Health team. In this exciting role, you will contribute to excellence in teaching and learning across the suite of cultural education topics, as well as contributing to teaching and the scholarship of teaching using innovations in teaching practices and technologies to deliver excellence in teaching. The successful candidate will possess cultural authority and will deliver education relating to Indigenous knowledges and cultural practices and will facilitate academic and logistic support to Indigenous students in the College and will work with members of the Indigenous Health Education Team to play a role in Flinders engagement with community, including consultation.   You will also have the exciting opportunity to: Create, coordinate and conduct inspiring teaching informed by approaches that enhance the student experience, with a focus on cultural education topics, promoting the principles of primary health care, cultural safety and an interprofessional approach to Indigenous Health education, across all levels and modes of the College. Contribute to the planning and evaluation of these topics, working with Topic and Course Coordinators as required. Make an independent contribution to the review, renewal, development and design of curricula and courses that are attractive to existing and new cohorts of students and that nurture creativity and critical thinking skills.   About You (Level A)   You are an emerging teaching professional who has experience in teaching and learning outputs in the areas of Indigenous health issues, cultural safety, primary health care and population health, and is ready to use your skillset in a tertiary educational environment. Your passion for cultural education, paired with your deep understanding of the nexus between teaching and learning, research and scholarship effortlessly enables you to create a nurturing and student-centred learning experience. Your continued commitment to community recognition of Indigenous knowledges and practices further highlights your sheer passion for sharing Cultural knowledge. You are also actively connected to community and/or having experience working within Aboriginal services and organisations that allow you to bring a deeper, sharper understanding of cultural safety, community perspectives and the realities of working in these spaces, effortless strengthening the quality and relevancy of your teaching and connection to students.   To succeed in this role, you will also be able to demonstrate the following: Satisfactory completion of 4 years tertiary study or equivalent qualifications and experience in the area of teaching For Indigenous staff members whose duties involve cultural activity which prioritises Indigenous knowledges and practices requiring community cultural recognition: evidence of such recognition plus completion or near completion of a higher degree study or substantive experience in some branch of Indigenous studies/education. Lived experience and/or experience working within Aboriginal communities, as well as established relationships with relevant groups and organisations, including the ability to lead community consultation. Demonstrates a commitment to fostering a positive workplace culture, and role modelling behaviours that support the University’s Reconciliation Action Plan (RAP) and commitment to enhancing educational opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Evidence of successful learning and teaching delivery or design, including use of e-learning technologies, as well as the engage with students (and deliver teaching modules) via online learning platforms.   About You (Level B)   You are an established teaching professional who has significant experience in teaching and learning outcomes at a tertiary level and is renowned for your excellence in teaching that is informed by approaches that genuinely enhance the student experience. Your experience in the independent innovation in learning and teaching delivery and/or design, inclusive of e-learning technologies, allows you to meet students where they are on their learning journey, regardless of their personal or professional demands. You bring knowledge from evidenced professional development of teaching, including peer or supervisor reviews of teaching and/or external accreditation processes, strengthening the quality of your teaching outcomes.   You are also actively connected to community and/or having experience working within Aboriginal services and organisations that allow you to bring a deeper, sharper understanding of cultural safety, community perspectives and the realities of working in these spaces, effortless strengthening the quality and relevancy of your teaching and connection to students. In addition to this, your experience will also highlight the following: Completion of a PhD or EdD, or equivalent qualification For Indigenous staff members whose duties involve cultural activity which prioritises Indigenous knowledges and practices requiring community cultural recognition: evidence of such recognition plus completion or near completion of a higher degree study or substantive experience in some branch of Indigenous studies/education. Lived experience and/or experience working within Aboriginal communities, as well as established relationships with relevant groups and organisations, including the ability to lead community consultation. Demonstrates a commitment to fostering a positive workplace culture, and role modelling behaviours that support the University’s Reconciliation Action Plan (RAP) and commitment to enhancing educational opportunities for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people. Demonstrated contribution to academic quality assurance processes, such as topic or course review, and experience in coordinating and/or leading the teaching activities of other staff. Experience with engagement within a relevant external professional domain and within a wider community context at local, national or international level which could include working parties, committees, professional associations and conferenced workshops.   This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters , by 2035 through the Value of Excellence. This value is demonstrated through the delivery of high-quality, innovative, and culturally responsive teaching that the Associate Lecturer / Lecturer works towards. The role advances student learning outcomes, contributes to the scholarship of teaching, supports Indigenous student success, and strengthens community engagement, reflecting a commitment to excellence in education, cultural leadership, and continuous improvement.   College of Medicine and Public Health             At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do.   Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.   Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.   Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.   Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Genuine flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.   These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy.   How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities for either the Level A Position Description or Level B Position Description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Larissa Taylor . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 03 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Coordinator-International-Pathways---Partnerships_JR0000017821": {
      "title": "Coordinator International Pathways & Partnerships",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nFull-Time | Fixed Term (12 months) Higher Education Officer Level 7 | $102,589 - $113,561 Location:  Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description    About the Role   Join a growing team and lead the identification, development, establishment and strategic review of international education pathways and articulation arrangements that support student access and progression to Flinders University programs, including transnational education and branch campus offerings.   The position works collaboratively with international schools, pathway providers, education institutions, Colleges, Global Engagement, Admissions and Compliance, Marketing and Student Recruitment, and other relevant stakeholders to assess opportunities and develop pathway models aligned with University priorities, academic requirements, partnership commitments and recruitment objectives and provides specialist advice on pathway design, articulation, credit and progression models.   You will be accountable for pathway opportunity development, design & coordination, performance & portfolio review and partnership development & strategic engagement, which will include the establishment, approval readiness and handover of these pathways.   For a more comprehensive list of key position responsibilities, please view the Position Description   This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to \"Changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters\", by 2035 through providing specialist advice on pathway design, articulation, credit and progression models; evaluation of pathway and arrangement performance; and recommendations for opportunities for enhancement, growth or risk treatment. It establishes operational requirements for approved arrangements and coordinates their structured handover to the Coordinator, Transnational Education and Partner Services   Working collaboratively with colleagues, academic staff and education partners, the role contributes to a vibrant culture and embraces an agile approach to delivering responsive, high-quality services in a dynamic environment.   About You   As success in this role requires an inclusive and collaborative approach, sound judgement, intercultural capability and a strong focus on innovation, evidence-informed decision-making and continuous improvement, you will possess exemplary high level communication, interpersonal, consultation and negotiation skills, including the ability to influence outcomes and work effectively across organisational and cultural boundaries.   You have proven experience developing, establishing or reviewing education pathways, articulation arrangements, partnership programs or related international education initiatives, and can demonstrate experience building productive relationships with schools, education institutions, pathway providers, government agencies and other relevant stakeholders.   To be successful in this role, you will bring: Demonstrated experience building productive relationships with schools, education institutions, pathway providers, government agencies and other relevant stakeholders. The ability to interpret and apply academic, admissions, pathway-related and regulatory requirements and translate them into workable arrangements Proven coordinate complex activities involving multiple stakeholders and achieve outcomes through collaboration and sound judgement. Well-developed analytical and problem-solving skills, including the ability to evaluate performance, identify opportunities and risks, and recommend practical solutions. Additional language capability relevant to the University’s international education partner regions is desirable.     About the   Office of the Deputy Vice Chancellor (Students)   The Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Students) provides administrative support to key strategic and operational functions of the University’s student-related and academic services. Under the general portfolio, the Office team support Portfolio leadership and facilitate dialogue between key service areas to help the Portfolio ensure Flinders’ educational offerings are innovative, of assured quality, and are student centred. This drives our values and ethos, our impact and engagement, and our vision to be internationally recognised as a world leader in research, an innovator in contemporary education, and the source of Australia’s most enterprising graduates.   Life at Flinders      They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.    Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.    Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.     So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice       Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment      Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.     Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan     Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy        Prescribed Conditions for Employment      Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University.  This includes A current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University.   The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.     How to Apply and Information   You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document, which addresses the below Key Position Capabilities of the  position description: Demonstrated experience developing, establishing or reviewing education pathways, articulation arrangements, partnership programs or related international education initiatives. Demonstrated experience building productive relationships with schools, education institutions, pathway providers, government agencies and other relevant stakeholders. Well developed to high level communication, interpersonal, consultation and negotiation skills, including the ability to influence outcomes and work effectively across organisational and cultural boundaries. Proven ability to coordinate complex activities involving multiple stakeholders and achieve outcomes through collaboration and sound judgement. Demonstrated ability to interpret and apply academic, admissions, pathway-related and regulatory requirements and translate them into workable arrangements. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through  recruitment@flinders.edu.au  or call 08 8201 2383.  For more information regarding this position, please contact  Chris Brebner Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 02 Nov 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
